    "gold_solution": "```python\nimport math\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Facility_Location\")\n    m.setParam(\"TimeLimit\", 100)\n\n    # Problem Data\n    facilities = list(range(5))  # F0..F4\n    customers = list(range(10))  # C0..C9\n\n    # Facility coordinates (x, y)\n    facility_coords = [(30, 83), (43, 83), (32, 2), (3, 50), (71, 96)]\n\n    # Customer coordinates (x, y)\n    customer_coords = [(96, 24), (89, 61), (90, 16), (35, 71), (13, 14), (34, 48), (79, 25), (72, 66), (72, 44), (16, 3)]\n\n    facility_capacities = [224.0, 160.0, 135.0, 113.0, 154.0]\n    customer_demands = [27.0, 18.0, 15.0, 25.0, 21.0, 48.0, 50.0, 16.0, 50.0, 21.0]\n    fixed_costs = [428.0, 368.0, 196.0, 259.0, 340.0]\n    transport_rate = 0.55  # $/km/unit\n\n    # COMPUTE distance matrix from coordinates (INDUCED COSTS)\n    def euclidean_distance(coord1, coord2):\n        return math.sqrt((coord1[0] - coord2[0])**2 + (coord1[1] - coord2[1])**2)\n\n    distances = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        for j in customers:\n            distances[(i, j)] = euclidean_distance(facility_coords[i], customer_coords[j])\n\n    # Decision Variables\n    # Open_F{i}: Binary - whether facility i is opened\n    open_facility = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        open_facility[i] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Open_F{i}\")\n\n    # Ship_F{i}_C{j}: Continuous - units shipped from facility i to customer j\n    ship = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        for j in customers:\n            ship[(i, j)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Ship_F{i}_C{j}\")\n\n    # Objective: Minimize fixed costs + transport costs\n    # Transport cost = distance * rate * units shipped\n    obj = quicksum(fixed_costs[i] * open_facility[i] for i in facilities)\n    obj += quicksum(\n        distances[(i, j)] * transport_rate * ship[(i, j)]\n        for i in facilities for j in customers\n    )\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # Demand satisfaction constraints\n    for j in customers:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(ship[(i, j)] for i in facilities) >= customer_demands[j],\n            name=f\"Demand_C{j}\"\n        )\n\n    # Capacity constraints (can only ship if facility is open)\n    for i in facilities:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(ship[(i, j)] for j in customers) <= facility_capacities[i] * open_facility[i],\n            name=f\"Capacity_F{i}\"\n        )\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "Subject: Warehouse Opening and Customer Service Plan\n\nWe\u2019re at a decision point on our distribution footprint. We have five candidate warehouse sites and ten customer locations to serve. The candidate site map points are in Facility Coordinates:\n  - F0: (30, 83)\n  - F1: (43, 83)\n  - F2: (32, 2)\n  - F3: (3, 50)\n  - F4: (71, 96), and the customer locations are provided in Customer Coordinates:\n  - C0: (96, 24)\n  - C1: (89, 61)\n  - C2: (90, 16)\n  - C3: (35, 71)\n  - C4: (13, 14)\n  - C5: (34, 48)\n  - C6: (79, 25)\n  - C7: (72, 66)\n  - C8: (72, 44)\n  - C9: (16, 3).\n\nHere\u2019s the business context:\n\n- Opening any warehouse requires a one-time commitment; the associated fixed costs are captured in [428.0, 368.0, 196.0, 259.0, 340.0]. A facility can only ship if it\u2019s opened.\n- Each site has a practical throughput limit; the maximum handling capacities are listed in [224.0, 160.0, 135.0, 113.0, 154.0].\n- Each customer has an expected order volume that must be met in full; those demands are detailed in [27.0, 18.0, 15.0, 25.0, 21.0, 48.0, 50.0, 16.0, 50.0, 21.0].\n- Moving product incurs freight expense that grows with distance and volume; our transport rate is 0.55.\n\nThe decision we need to make is which warehouses to open and how to allocate shipments from the open sites to each customer so that every customer\u2019s demand is satisfied without exceeding any facility\u2019s capacity, while minimizing total cost. Total cost here combines the one-time opening commitments with the ongoing shipping spend driven by how far we move product and how much we ship.\n\nKey trade-offs to consider:\n\n- Opening more warehouses can shorten routes and reduce freight but increases fixed overhead; opening fewer sites saves on fixed costs but may lengthen routes and risk capacity bottlenecks.\n- Closest-site serving is often cheaper, but we need to balance loads so no single warehouse becomes constrained.\n- If a single site cannot efficiently cover nearby customers due to capacity limits, we may split customer orders across multiple open warehouses to maintain service reliability.\n\nWhat we aim to deliver:\n\n- A recommended set of warehouses to open now, with a clear rationale that balances cost, service levels, and operational resilience.\n- An assignment plan showing which customers are served by which open warehouses and the approximate volumes along each lane, aligned with the rules above.\n- A transparent view of projected total cost under the recommended plan versus plausible alternatives.\n\nOnce we align on these objectives and rules, we\u2019ll produce a plan that minimizes total cost while protecting service performance and capacity headroom.",
    "LLM_description_template": "Subject: Warehouse network decision and customer fulfillment plan\n\nTeam,\n\nWe need to decide which of our five candidate warehouse sites to open and how to route shipments to our ten customer locations for the upcoming cycle. Our aim is to cover every customer\u2019s needs while keeping total spend as low as possible.\n\nThe five candidate facility locations are provided in {FACILITY_COORDINATES}, and the ten customer locations are listed in {CUSTOMER_COORDINATES}. Opening any warehouse triggers a fixed cost as outlined in {FIXED_COSTS}. Each site has a ceiling on how much volume it can handle, detailed in {FACILITY_CAPACITIES}, and each customer has a firm requirement that must be fully supplied, shown in {CUSTOMER_DEMANDS}. Transportation is charged at {TRANSPORT_RATE}, so longer routes or higher volumes will drive higher freight spend.\n\nKey business rules:\n- A facility can only ship if it\u2019s opened.\n- No facility can exceed its capacity.\n- Every customer\u2019s demand must be fully satisfied.\n\nThe decision comes down to a balance. Opening more warehouses increases fixed costs but can shorten average delivery distances and potentially reduce freight spend. Opening fewer sites saves on fixed costs but may push us toward longer-haul shipping. Capacity limits may require splitting customer demand across multiple open sites, and some locations are naturally better positioned to serve nearby customer clusters.\n\nOur objective is to minimize total cost\u2014the sum of facility opening costs and transportation spend\u2014while meeting all customer demand under the capacity limits. We will evaluate combinations of sites to open and assign customer volumes to the open locations in a way that delivers the lowest total cost and a practical, executable shipping plan. Once we identify the recommended network, we\u2019ll share the rationale, the cost breakdown, and implications for operations and service levels."
